{"id": "62dfb4bda6", "class": "B", "edits": [{"find": "A medical degree is not the same thing as a licence to work as a doctor. In Denmark the licence is called autorisation", "replace": "A medical degree is not the same thing as a licence to work as a doctor, and the licence comes from the country where you want to practise. This section uses Denmark as the example. In Denmark the licence is called autorisation"}, {"find": "may still be recognised under the automatic-recognition principles.", "replace": "may still be recognised under the automatic-recognition principles.\n\nThe Directive applies across the EU/EEA, so an Annex V degree is recognised automatically if you plan to practise in Germany, Czechia or anywhere else in the EU/EEA too. Each country's own medical authority issues the licence and sets its own language and paperwork checks, so ask the authority where you want to practise."}], "appliesTo": "Readers planning to practise in Denmark; the Directive applies in every EU/EEA country", "needsVerification": true, "verifyNote": "Wording of the added paragraph rests on Directive 2005/36/EC Art. 21 and Art. 53 (language knowledge may be checked by host states); confirm before publishing and do not name country-specific tests unless sourced.", "note": null}
